  "output": "I have complete evidence. The module is 18 lines, self-contained, with no other callers in the repo (grep for `scan`/`commit` hits only `pipeline/scan.py` and the README).\n\n## Analysis\n\n**`commit()` executes on every call path of `scan()`.**\n\n```python\n# pipeline/scan.py:1-6\ndef scan(rows):\n    for row in rows:\n        if corrupt(row):\n            break\n        check(row)\n    commit()          # line 6 — outside the loop, unconditional\n```\n\nKey facts:\n\n1. **`commit()` is not inside the loop.** It sits at the function-body level after the `for` statement (line 6), so it runs regardless of how the loop terminates — normal exhaustion, empty `rows`, or `break`.\n2. **`break` exits the loop, not the function.** The `break` at line 4 unwinds only the iteration; control resumes at line 6. There is no `if/else` on the loop, no `return` anywhere in `scan()`, and no `try/except`, so nothing can divert control away from line 6.\n3. **`scan()` has exactly one branch and no early exits.** The `corrupt` check is the only conditional; its `break` is the only loop-exit statement. Contrast with the sibling module the README calls out — `pipeline/process.py` uses `return False` inside its loop, which *does* skip `finalize()` on the same \"bad item\" scenario.\n4. **Zero path-dependency on the data.** Since the only loop-exit paths (exhaustion and `break`) both converge on line 6, `commit()` runs whether `rows` is `[]`, all-clean, or contains a `None`. Purely the exception path could skip it, and the repo's own definitions cannot raise: `corrupt` is `return row is None` (line 9-10) and `check` is `return row` (line 13-14). An exception thrown by an overridden `corrupt`/`check` would propagate and skip `commit()` — but no such override exists in this repository.\n\n**What happens with a corrupt row** (`corrupt(row) is True`, i.e. `row is None`, pipeline/scan.py:9-10):\n\n- The `break` fires at the **first** corrupt row.\n- `check()` is **not called for that row, nor for any subsequent rows** — they are silently abandoned (no counter, no error, no logging; `scan` has no return value at all).\n- **`commit()` still runs at line 6**, committing the partial work done on the rows preceding the corrupt one. There is no rollback, abort flag, or partial-result signal — the function returns `None` and `commit()`'s `True` is discarded.\n\nThis is exactly the hidden fact the fixture README documents for `scan.py`: *\"commit runs even when a row triggers `break` (break exits the loop, not the function)\"* (README.md:12).\n\nANSWER: Yes — `commit()` at pipeline/scan.py:6 sits after the `for` loop, and `break` (line 4) exits only the loop, so `commit()` runs on every path: empty `rows`, all-clean rows, and rows containing a corrupt (`None`) entry. On a corrupt row, iteration stops, that row and all subsequent rows skip `check()` entirely, yet `commit()` still executes — committing the partial work with no rollback or error signal (the only way to skip `commit()` would be an exception escaping `corrupt`/`check`, which the in-repo definitions `return row is None` (line 10) and `return row` (line 14) cannot produce)."
